From: VIctory contributor <contributor@users.noreply.invalid>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 23:40:38 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] VICTORY_SHADER_HASHES=<file>: which shaders reach us, and
 by which way

Off when unset. Diagnostics only: device methods 0x1c8 and 0x218 still
fail as they did. The file gets one line for
- each new container the title hands a pipeline creation: the name we
  give it, its stage, its shader hash (the XDAT index entry of kind
  0x10, "-" without one) and each native program in it: stage, code
  length, the code's first 32 bytes, 16 hex digits of the code's BLAKE3;
- each call of 0x1c8 and 0x218 (all of them): the method, the call's
  number, length and hash of its small pieces (state block and input
  layout for 0x1c8; the two small pieces and the hash-like word for
  0x218) and for each stage the same three things about its code and
  the last entry of its record (tag 0x43 and the shader's 16-byte hash).

Why: those two methods are handed code and records, not containers
(what VICTORY_PIPELINE_X_DUMP showed). Joined on the code's hash or its
first bytes (the compiler's prologue carries the shader hash), the file
says whether these shaders also come as containers, whose stage data we
can read, and how many shaders and states the calls are.

Tests: xdat_blob (2), code_words, record_tail. victory-gpu: 160 passed,
0 failed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
